     "type": "application",
     "title": "Building a Nightly Mind Sweep",
     "content": "Turn the research into a habit by running a short mind sweep before bed or first thing in the morning, whichever fits your schedule better, rather than relying only on the weekly review. Set a timer for ten minutes and write down every open commitment currently occupying background attention, without editing or judging how big or small it is: a birthday gift you have not bought, a conversation you have been avoiding, a subscription you meant to cancel. The goal is quantity and honesty, not neatness.\n\nThen, and this is the step the research shows actually matters, go back through the list and attach a specific next action and, where possible, a time to each item. Do not stop at naming the task; decide the very next physical step and roughly when you will take it. An item that says pick a restaurant for Mom's birthday is not yet resolved in the way the research requires; look up three options online tomorrow at lunch is. If an item genuinely has no next action right now, mark it explicitly as someday and note when you will next reconsider it, since even that small act of deciding not to decide yet reduces its pull compared to leaving it fully unresolved.\n\nKeep this list somewhere you trust completely, whether that is a notebook or an app, because the psychological benefit depends on believing the system will actually remind you, not merely on the act of writing. Over a few weeks of consistent practice, most people report the daytime hum of low-grade anxiety Allen describes noticeably quiets, not because the workload shrank, but because working memory is no longer being asked to double as a to-do list.",
